%U A396945 16,16,24,16,4,72,4,16,32,14,16,48,8,12,36,48,4
%N A396945 Number of divisors of n^3 + 1.
%C A396945 Because n^3 + 1 factors into (n+1) * (n^2-n+1), the number of divisors a(n) depends on the prime factorizations of these two polynomials. The greatest common divisor of n+1 and n^2-n+1 is always either 1 or 3. Because of this tight restriction, the sequence splits almost entirely multiplicatively, which distinguishes it from an arbitrary composition of two sequences.
%F A396945 a(n) = A000005(A001093(n)).
%e A396945 For n = 3, n^3 + 1 = 28. The divisors of 28 are 1, 2, 4, 7, 14, and 28. Because there are 6 divisors, a(3) = 6.
%e A396945 For n = 5, n^3 + 1 = 126. The divisors of 126 are 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 9, 14, 18, 21, 42, 63, and 126. Because there are 12 divisors, a(5) = 12.
%p A396945 seq(numtheory[tau](n^3 + 1), n=0..40);
%t A396945 Table[DivisorSigma[0, n^3 + 1], {n, 0, 40}]
%o A396945 (PARI) a(n) = numdiv(n^3 + 1);
%o A396945 (Python)
%o A396945 from sympy import divisor_count
%o A396945 def A_new(n):
%o A396945     return divisor_count(n**3 + 1)
%o A396945 print([A_new(n) for n in range(41)])
%Y A396945 Cf. A000005 (number of divisors of n), A001093 (n^3 + 1), A005069 (number of divisors of n^2 + 1).
%K A396945 nonn,easy,new
